      title: "Bernard and the Cistercians",
      body: `## A new monasticism

In the late eleventh and twelfth centuries, Latin Christendom experienced a religious revival that produced new forms of religious life. Many people, laity and clergy alike, felt that the existing institutions of the Church, even the reformed monasteries such as Cluny, had grown too rich, too comfortable, and too entangled with the world. They sought a more authentic Christian life, modeled on the **apostles** and the desert fathers, characterized by **poverty**, simplicity, manual labor, and withdrawal from the world. This search for the "**apostolic life**" (*vita apostolica*) took many forms: hermits living in forests, wandering preachers, communities of canons living by the Rule of Saint Augustine, and new monastic orders.

## Cîteaux

The most influential of the new orders was the **Cistercian** order, named after its first house, **Cîteaux** (Latin *Cistercium*), founded in **1098** in a marshy wilderness in Burgundy by **Robert of Molesme** and a group of monks who had left their monastery seeking a stricter observance of the **Rule of Saint Benedict**. The Cistercians rejected the elaborate liturgy, rich decoration, and wealth of Cluny. They wore undyed habits of rough wool—hence their name, the "**White Monks**," as opposed to the Benedictine "Black Monks." They ate a simple vegetarian diet, worked with their hands, and built their monasteries in remote places, "far from the habitation of men," as their statutes required.

The Cistercians developed a new form of organization, set out in a document called the **Charter of Charity** (*Carta Caritatis*), attributed to Abbot **Stephen Harding**. Each new monastery was founded by a "mother house," whose abbot visited it every year to ensure that it observed the rule. All the abbots of the order met annually in a **General Chapter** at Cîteaux, which issued statutes binding on all houses. This combination of autonomy and central supervision made the Cistercians the first true monastic **order**, and a model for later religious orders.

## Lay brothers and the land

The Cistercians also admitted **lay brothers** (*conversi*), usually men of peasant origin, who took vows but did not become full monks; they did most of the agricultural work, living in separate quarters and following a simpler round of prayer. With their labor, the Cistercians cleared forests, drained marshes, and developed new lands. They became pioneers of agriculture, especially of sheep farming: the great Cistercian abbeys of Yorkshire, such as **Rievaulx** and **Fountains**, became major producers of wool, exported to the cloth towns of Flanders. Ironically, their efficiency made many Cistercian houses very rich, and by the thirteenth century critics accused them of the same greed they had once condemned in others.

## Bernard

The Cistercian order owed its astonishing expansion above all to one man: **Bernard of Clairvaux** (1090–1153). A young Burgundian noble, Bernard entered Cîteaux in **1113** with some thirty friends and relatives, whom he had persuaded to join him—an event that saved the struggling community. In **1115**, at the age of twenty-five, he was sent to found a new house at **Clairvaux**, where he remained abbot until his death. Under his influence, the order grew explosively: at his death in 1153, there were some 340 Cistercian houses across Europe, sixty-eight of them founded directly from Clairvaux.

Bernard was the most influential churchman of his age. He was a mystic, a theologian, a preacher of extraordinary power, and a tireless writer of letters to popes, kings, and nobles. His sermons on the **Song of Songs**, interpreting the biblical love poem as an allegory of the love between Christ and the soul, are masterpieces of mystical literature. He promoted devotion to the Virgin Mary and to the humanity of Christ. He also intervened in politics: he resolved a disputed papal election in 1130 in favor of Innocent II, wrote the rule of the Templars, preached the Second Crusade, and condemned the theologian Peter Abelard, whom we will meet in the next lesson. One of his own monks became Pope **Eugenius III** (1145–1153), to whom Bernard addressed a treatise, *On Consideration*, warning him against the dangers of papal power and worldly business.

## The Apologia

Bernard's view of monastic life is vividly expressed in his ***Apologia*** (c. 1125), addressed to William of Saint-Thierry, in which he attacked the luxury of Cluniac monasteries. He condemned their rich food and clothing, and above all their art: their enormous churches, their golden reliquaries, their paintings and sculptures. "What is the point," he asked, "in the cloisters, where the brothers are reading, of those ridiculous monstrosities, that deformed beauty and beautiful deformity? What are those unclean apes, fierce lions, monstrous centaurs, half-men? … You may see many bodies under one head, and many heads on one body. … One could spend the whole day gazing at these things rather than meditating on the law of God. For God's sake, if they are not ashamed of the folly, why do they not at least regret the expense?"

The passage is both a condemnation of Romanesque sculpture and, ironically, one of the most vivid descriptions of it. Cistercian churches, by contrast, were austere and unadorned, with plain walls, clear glass, and no figurative sculpture, relying on light, proportion, and fine stonework for their beauty. Their architecture, which spread across Europe, helped prepare the way for the Gothic style.

## The Cistercian ideal

The Cistercians embodied a central paradox of medieval religious life: the search for poverty and withdrawal from the world produced institutions that became wealthy and powerful, and the holiest men of the age, like Bernard, became deeply involved in the affairs of the world. This paradox would reappear, in even sharper form, among the friars of the thirteenth century.

> **Pause and consider.** Bernard condemned the "ridiculous monstrosities" of Cluniac sculpture while describing them vividly. What does his critique reveal about the relationship between art and spiritual life?`,
